      en: "The Tessenei Lowland is a vast alluvial plain in western Eritrea, formed by the Gash River. Historically, it has been a crossroads for trade routes connecting the Red Sea to the Ethiopian highlands. The area was part of the Kingdom of Aksum and later contested during the Eritrean War of Independence. Today, it is a key agricultural region with a population of over 200,000. The lowland is also known for its archaeological sites, including ancient settlements and tombs. The Gash River, though seasonal, supports irrigation and sustains local livelihoods.",
